{
  "concept": "As a shallow one-hidden-layer network is given more hidden units, its fitted function gains enough piecewise-linear regions to pass through the training data AND track the true underlying function — capacity, not overfitting, is the limiting factor here.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "3×2 grid of six panels labelled a) b) c) d) e) f)",
    "Panel titles: (a) no title / ground-truth only, (b) '6 hidden units', (c) '7 hidden units', (d) '8 hidden units', (e) '10 hidden units', (f) '50 hidden units'",
    "Shared x-axis labelled 'Input, x' with ticks 0.0, 0.5, 1.0 (bottom row panels carry the label)",
    "Shared y-axis labelled 'Output, y' with ticks -1.0, 0.0, 1.0 (left column panels carry the label)",
    "Black thick smooth curve: the true underlying function (one large peak near x≈0.25, trough near x≈0.75, rising slightly at x=1), identical in all six panels",
    "Salmon/terracotta filled circles: 7 training data points, identical positions in all panels — three on the rising left flank (x≈0.03, 0.12, 0.19), one below the trough (x≈0.81), two on the right flank (x≈0.92, 0.97)",
    "Pale teal/mint piecewise-linear curve: the fitted network output, present in panels b–f only, with a different number of visible kinks per panel",
    "Panel b fit: wildly diverging linear segments that shoot off the top and bottom of the axes between x≈0.3 and x≈0.8",
    "Panel c fit: overshooting peak above the true peak, deep dip below the axis near x≈0.72",
    "Panel d fit: peak slightly too high and too wide, dip too deep near x≈0.75",
    "Panel e fit: close to the true curve with a slight rightward shift on the falling flank",
    "Panel f fit: nearly indistinguishable from the true curve except for a mild bulge on the falling flank",
    "Axis frames drawn as plain black rectangles (box) with clipped curves",
    "Legend/key mapping: black = true function, teal = network fit, salmon dots = training data"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "capacity",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That each extra hidden unit adds another linear region (another kink), and that the fit goes from violently unconstrained, to kinked but roughly right, to essentially exact as the number of regions exceeds what the data and function demand.",
      "label": "Hidden units:  — · 6 · 7 · 8 · 10 · 50",
      "range": [0, 5, 1],
      "default": 0
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the jump from 6 to 50 units is not a small refinement but the difference between an uncontrolled and a near-perfect approximation, and that seeing all capacities side by side reveals a monotone trend rather than random variation.",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Single panel", "Compare 6 vs 50", "All six panels"],
      "default": "Single panel"
    },
    {
      "id": "pickPanel",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That any one capacity can be isolated and inspected against the seven training points, so the learner can check whether the fit actually interpolates the data or merely comes close."
    },
    {
      "id": "hoverPoint",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "The vertical residual between the fitted curve and each training point at the current capacity, making 'fits the data' a measurable quantity rather than a visual impression.",
      "teaches_note": "hover over a salmon data point or a kink in the teal curve"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The target and the data",
      "narration": "A smooth black true function and just seven salmon training points — this is everything the network gets to see. Notice the points are clustered on the two flanks and say almost nothing about the trough.",
      "state": { "capacity": 0, "viewMode": "Single panel" }
    },
    {
      "title": "6 hidden units: too few regions",
      "narration": "With six units the fit has only a handful of linear pieces, and it uses them to plunge far off the plot between the data clusters. It can hit the points but the function in between is nonsense.",
      "state": { "capacity": 1, "viewMode": "Single panel" }
    },
    {
      "title": "7 and 8 units: the shape appears",
      "narration": "One or two more units buy enough kinks to produce a peak and a trough in roughly the right places, though both overshoot the true curve badly.",
      "state": { "capacity": 3, "viewMode": "Single panel" }
    },
    {
      "title": "10 units: close, with a visible lag",
      "narration": "Ten units track the true function almost everywhere; the only obvious flaw is a slight rightward drift on the falling flank where no data constrain it.",
      "state": { "capacity": 4, "viewMode": "Single panel" }
    },
    {
      "title": "50 units: more parameters, better — not worse",
      "narration": "With fifty hidden units and still only seven data points the fit does not wiggle wildly; it hugs the true function. Extra capacity bought smoothness, not overfitting.",
      "state": { "capacity": 5, "viewMode": "Single panel" }
    },
    {
      "title": "6 versus 50 side by side",
      "narration": "Put the extremes next to each other: same data, same training procedure, radically different behaviour between the data points.",
      "state": { "capacity": 5, "viewMode": "Compare 6 vs 50" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The whole ladder",
      "narration": "All six panels at once show a clean monotone trend — error between the data points falls steadily as hidden units increase, with no sign of the classical overfitting bulge.",
      "state": { "capacity": 5, "viewMode": "All six panels" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Adding hidden units far beyond the number of training points makes the fitted function smoother and closer to the truth, not wilder — the danger of a small shallow network is not too little smoothing but too few linear regions to behave sensibly where there is no data.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Six equal panels in a 3-wide, 2-tall grid on white, each with a plain black box frame. Every panel plots the identical thick black true curve (a smooth wave: rises from y≈-0.3 at x=0 to a peak y≈0.6 near x=0.25, falls to a trough y≈-0.65 near x=0.75, then rises slightly to y≈-0.3 at x=1) and the identical seven salmon/terracotta filled circles (three on the left rising flank, one just below the trough near x≈0.81, two on the right flank near x≈0.92 and 0.97). Panel a has no fit and no title; panels b–f carry titles '6 / 7 / 8 / 10 / 50 hidden units' and add a pale mint-teal piecewise-linear fit of comparable thickness drawn on top of, but not obscuring, the black curve. The teal fit must always pass through or extremely near all seven salmon points; what changes with capacity is behaviour between them: at 6 units it shoots vertically off both the top and bottom of the axes in the data-free middle region; at 7 it overshoots the peak and dips below -1 near x≈0.72; at 8 it has a too-tall, too-wide peak and a too-deep trough; at 10 it is close with a rightward-shifted falling flank; at 50 it nearly coincides with the black curve with only a mild bulge on the falling flank. Axes: x ticks 0.0/0.5/1.0 labelled 'Input, x' on the bottom row, y ticks -1.0/0.0/1.0 labelled 'Output, y' on the left column; curves are clipped at the frame. Panel letters a)–f) sit at the upper-left outside each frame in bold. Colour key: black = true function, mint-teal = network fit, salmon = training data."
}

